{"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Afluent\/documentation\/Afluent\/BackoffJitter","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"sections":[],"kind":"symbol","schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"major":0,"minor":3},"abstract":[{"text":"How a wait is randomized, so many clients that started waiting together don’t all wake together.","type":"text"}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/afluent\/backoffjitter"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"BackoffJitter","kind":"identifier"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Jitter takes a nominal wait and picks the wait to use instead. It works on any clock’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"Duration","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", and the operators that wait take it as a "},{"code":"jitter:","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" parameter:"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Afluent\/documentation\/Afluent\/ExponentialBackoffStrategy","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" (each backoff delay), "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Afluent\/documentation\/Afluent\/AsyncSequences\/TimerSequence","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" (where"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"each tick falls inside its period) and "},{"code":"delay","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" on units of work and sequences (each wait)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The kinds follow “Exponential Backoff and Jitter” (AWS Architecture Blog):","type":"text"}]},{"items":[{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Afluent\/documentation\/Afluent\/BackoffJitter\/none","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":": the nominal wait,